## 3. Leader killed (kill -9)
   concept: a dead leader cannot release anything; the followers wait until expires_at passes, so failover costs up to the TTL plus one renew interval, and the job does not run meanwhile
   00:54:50 kill -9 c (pid 12728)
   00:54:52 [b] acquired the lease, term 2; c's lease had expired 0.1s ago
   00:54:52 [b] job ran, term 2
   00:54:53 b holds the lease
   00:54:53 [a] follower, b leads (term 2)
   00:54:53 [b] job ran, term 2
   00:54:54 [b] job ran, term 2
   00:54:55 [b] job ran, term 2

## 4. Leader paused right after a renew: self-fencing
   concept: the leader freezes past its TTL; a follower takes over with a higher term; on resume, the old leader's own check (last renew sent more than a TTL ago on its monotonic clock) stops the job before any write
   00:54:56 kill -USR2 b: stop right after the next renew
   00:54:56 [b] renewed term 2, before the job's lease check: SIGSTOP now (a GC pause stand-in)
   00:55:00 [a] acquired the lease, term 3; b's lease had expired 1.0s ago
   00:55:00 [a] job ran, term 3
   00:55:01 [a] job ran, term 3
   00:55:02 kill -CONT b, after a took over
   00:55:02 [b] SIGCONT: resumed
   00:55:02 [b] self-fenced: last renew was sent 5.9s ago, past the 3s TTL, so the lease may be someone else's; job skipped
   00:55:02 [a] job ran, term 3
   00:55:03 [b] renew refused: a holds term 3; stepping down
   00:55:03 [a] job ran, term 3
   00:55:04 [a] job ran, term 3

## 5. Paused after its check: fencing tokens
   concept: the pause lands between the lease check and the write, so the check was true and the stale write goes out; ticks takes it (two leaders wrote), fenced_ticks rejects it because it has already seen a higher term
   00:55:05 [a] job ran, term 3
   00:55:06 kill -USR1 a: stop right after the next lease check
   00:55:06 [a] lease check passed for term 3, before the write: SIGSTOP now (a GC pause stand-in)
